%I A217514 #22 Nov 26 2026 09:42:41
%S A217514 4,10,22,30,42,66,82,106,136,148,172,196,220,256,268,316,344,368,404,
%T A217514 452,476,512,542,578,610,634,706,730,790,838,874,922,950,974,1022,
%U A217514 1034,1130,1190,1214,1274,1346,1370,1418,1472,1520,1568,1604,1676,1724,1784
%N A217514 Partial sums of nonzero terms in A005927.
%C A217514 Sizes of clusters of atoms in diamond structure.
%C A217514 a(n) is the number of lattice points (x, y, z) in the diamond cubic structure (scaled so nearest-neighbor distance is sqrt(3)) within Euclidean distance sqrt(s(n)) of an unoccupied tetrahedral interstitial site (with s(n) as in Formula), counting points with x == y == z == 1 (mod 2) and x + y + z == 1 (mod 4) together with points with x == y == z == 0 (mod 2) and x + y + z == 2 (mod 4). - _Andrew J. Morris_, Nov 22 2026
%D A217514 N. J. A. Sloane, Theta-Series and Magic Numbers for Diamond and Certain Ionic Crystal Structures, J. Math. Phys., 28 (1987), pp. 1653-1657.
%H A217514 Andrew J. Morris, <a href="/A217514/b217514.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A217514 Andrew J. Morris, <a href="/A217514/a217514.txt">C++ program</a>
%F A217514 a(n) = Sum_{j=0..s(n)} A005927(j), where s(n) = 32*floor(n/7) + 4*r + 3*((r + 1) mod 2) with r = n mod 7. - _Andrew J. Morris_, Nov 22 2026
%F A217514 a(n) ~ Pi*(32*n/7)^(3/2)/6. - _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Nov 26 2026
%e A217514 For n = 0, s(0) = 3; the 4 nearest neighbors (+-1, +-1, +-1) with x + y + z == 1 (mod 4) give a(0) = 4.
%e A217514 For n = 1, s(1) = 4; adding the 6 axial neighbors (+-2, 0, 0), (0, +-2, 0), (0, 0, +-2) gives a(1) = 4 + 6 = 10.
%e A217514 For n = 2, s(2) = 11; adding the 12 permutations of (+-3, +-1, +-1) with x + y + z == 1 (mod 4) gives a(2) = 10 + 12 = 22.
%o A217514 (PARI) lista(m) = {s = 0; for (n=0, m, v = A005927(n); if (v != 0, s += v; print1(s, ", ");););} \\ _Michel Marcus_, Feb 17 2013
%o A217514 (C++) // See links.
%Y A217514 Cf. A005925-A005927, A217512-A217514.
%K A217514 nonn,changed
%O A217514 0,1
